<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hengistbury Head</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hengistbury Head is a headland on the south coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christchurch">Christchurch</a> Harbour, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>. In the 1st century BCE it was one of the main ports of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, trading with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armorica">Armorica</a> and receiving Italian wine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amphora">amphorae</a>, while exporting metals, grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. Excavations there have also found much older remains, including a late Ice Age hunters' camp.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hengistbury-head.jpg" alt="Hengistbury Head" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hut 8.5 at en.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hengistbury_Head.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
